      "is_best": false,
      "html": "<div class=\"post__content post__content--new-editor\"><p>Hello ​<user-mention data-id=\"108421\">@siva_28</user-mention> </p><p>STM32CubeMX mainly generates <strong>C code</strong>, but you can still use it very easily in a <strong>C++ project</strong> by generating the hardware initialization code with CubeMX, keeping those generated files unchanged as much as possible, and then adding your own <strong><code>.cpp</code></strong> and <strong><code>.hpp</code></strong> files for the application logic.</p><p>BR, Souhaib</p></div>"
    },
    {
      "is_best": true,
      "html": "<div class=\"post__content post__content--new-editor\"><p>Hello ​<user-mention data-id=\"108421\">@siva_28</user-mention> </p><p>First make sure <strong>STM32CubeIDE</strong> is selected as the toolchain</p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/59d0a8ff-14e1-4c20-869e-270c75d63280.png\"/></figure><p> </p><p>After generating the code with <strong>STM32CubeMX ,</strong> then open the project in <strong>STM32CubeIDE</strong>, right-click the project, and choose <strong>Convert to C++</strong>.</p><figure><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/84dbf04a-488d-40e8-ac89-13b7ae70d7b4.png\"/></figure><p> </p></div>"
    },
    {
      "is_best": false,
      "html": "<div class=\"post__content post__content--new-editor\"><p>Thank You for the reply, Now I can use in cpp</p></div>"
    }
  ]
